--------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. USE OF DATA

These data are made freely available to the public and the scientific
community in the belief that their wide dissemination will lead to greater
understanding and new scientific insights.  The availability of these
data does not constitute publication of the data.  We expect all users
to assure that the authors receive fair credit for their work.  If the
data are obtained for potential use in a publication or presentation
the authors should be informed at the outset of the nature of this work.
If the data are essential to the work, or if an important result or
conclusion depends on the data, co-authorship may be appropriate.
This should be discussed at an early stage in the work.  Manuscripts
using the data should be sent to the authors for review before they
are submitted for publication, so we can ensure that the quality and
limitations of the data are accurately represented.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. RECIPROCITY

Use of these data implies an agreement to reciprocate.  Individuals
making similar measurements agree to make their own data available to
the general public and to the scientific community in an equally complete
and easily accessible form.  Modelers are encouraged to make available to
the community, upon request, their own tools used in the interpretation
of this data, namely well-documented model code, transport fields, and
additional information necessary for other scientists to repeat the work
and to run modified versions.  Model availability includes collaborative
support for new users of the models.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. WARNINGS

Every effort is made to produce the most accurate and precise measurements
possible.  However, we reserve the right to make corrections to the data.
We are not responsible for results and conclusions based on use of these
data without regard to this warning.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. UPDATE NOTES

None.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. INTRODUCTION

These column-average Dry Mole Fractions (DMF) were retrieved from near-infrared
solar observations collected using a Fourier Transform Spectrometer (FTS).
Specifically an EM27/SUN spectrometer, instrument number [insert number here]
was used to collect the observations and GGG2020 was used to perform the
FFT and the trace gas retrieval.

[Consider describing setup here, who collected data, what were locations
were they collected as part of some effort such as a campaign, etc.]
[other details, especially about measurement site and overall duration
or intended duration of measurements]

Spectra were processed using the 2020 version of GGG created by
Geoffrey Toon. Details on GGG are available at
https://tccon-wiki.caltech.edu
EGI2020 was used to create inputs for GGG and automate the retrieval process.
Details on EGI are available at: https://tccon-wiki.caltech.edu

Column abundances of CO2, H2O, CH4, and O2 [add other gases if appropriate]
were retrieved. The O2 retrieval is used to convert all others into
DMFs. For example, dry column-average CO2 DMF has been calculated according to

CO2 DMF = 0.2095 x column CO2 / column O2.


These data may require some screening for clouds.  Some correction for
clouds has already been made by invoking the DC correction in the I2S
software.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. GENERAL COMMENTS ON DATA

[These notes were for a higher resolution TCCON instrument for an older
version of GGG and may not reflect current uncertainties.]

Column CO2 is retrieved for two CO2 bands centered at 6228 cm-1 and
6348 cm-1.  Column O2 is retrieved from the electronic band centered at
7882 cm-1.  The average of the two calculated CO2 DMFs is presented here.

The dry column-average CO2 DMF (0.2095 x column CO2 / column O2) is
affected by three main sources of error:

1. Measurement precision
The standard deviation of column CO2 / column O2 during a one hour period
is better than 0.1% under clear sky conditions and ~0.2% under partly
cloudy conditions.  Repeatability of the measurement is not a significant
source of error.

2. Spectroscopic errors
The retrieved O2 columns were NOT reduced by 2.27% to bring them into
agreement with the dry surface pressure, as described in Washenfelder et
al. (2006).  The absolute accuracy of the CO2 retrievals was calibrated
by comparison to integrated aircraft profiles, resulting in a correction
of 0.99.  The standard deviation of the fitting residuals is ~0.1%.
The aircraft profiles were performed with the sun
at airmass 1.1 to 2.0 (SZA 25 to 60 deg), and the column-average CO2 DMF is
now well-calibrated for these values.  However, additional systematic
errors may be present at higher airmass due to errors in the spectroscopy
of CO2 and or O2.

3. Systematic instrumental changes over time
Retrieved O2 DMF increased by ~0.3% during the observation period reported
here. We believe that this error affects both the CO2 and O2 retrievals
similarly, but for now assume that the column CO2 / column O2 ratio may
also have a systematic error of 0.3% over the observation period.

In summary, the measurement precision of ~0.1% under clear sky and
~0.2% under partly cloudy conditions does not affect the accuracy of the
measurements.  However, spectroscopic errors introduce a systematic bias
which depends on airmass.  We have calibrated the FTS column retrievals
at airmass 1.1 to 2.0 during Jul to Aug 2004, and expect that the absolute
accuracy at these airmasses has been maintained within 0.3% throughout
the subsequent data record.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. EM27/SUN BIASES
The lower resolution of EM27/SUN instruments affects the retrieval
of various gases, especially water.
We are not fully taking into account instrument line shapes (ILS). Methods
exist to measure the ILS (e.g., from ambient water lines) but have
generally shown a bias in the modulation efficiency, even for well-aligned
instruments.

There are 4 biases to consider when working with these data.
1. A constant offset that can vary by up to about 0.25% among retrievals from
different instruments. The scaling to e.g., TCCON may be larger.
2. Long term (multi-year) linear trends. These tend to be negligibly small for
XCO2, and is as small as about 0.05% for XCH4.
3. Be aware that studies agree on a season cycle of about 0.5% (~2 ppm) in the
XCO2 bias between EM27/SUN and TCCON observations, as well as about a
0.5% seasonal cycle in the bias of XCH4.
The origin of this changing seasonal bias remains remains unclear.
4. Within a given day the EM27/SUN to TCCON bias may vary by about 0.4-0.5%.


